WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
22 trillion one of wears stored South Korea's river water, then left it to still to breathe. This

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:12.920
is unintended consequences, episode 157. This is Lee Myeong-baks water security choice. The rushed

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:18.380
orders that fixed depth and storage, the still water and summer blooms, the audits that could not

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:24.080
unwind the target, and a question about testing flow before a number is locked. Lee Myeong-baks had

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.420
campaigned on water security and flood protection. The country had lived through years of drought and

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.420
typhoon damage, and those memories sat close to the surface of the election.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:39.440
In June 2008, two months after he abandoned a larger grand canal proposal amid public

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.640
opposition, he turned to the four major rivers as a practical alternative.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.060
He directed his land minister to add weirs and deepen dredging, rather than rely on

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.340
dyke reinforcement alone.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.720
Government planners had initially proposed reinforcing existing dykes and limited dredging

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.900
to manage peak flows, Lee believed deeper channels and controllable storage would deliver

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.380
both reliable supply and safety during the monsoon season.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.540
At the time that approach aligned with conventional engineering views, faster moving deeper water

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.440
was understood to reduce overflow risks for downstream cities and farms.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.640
The administration treated the work as a straightforward infrastructure upgrade.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.660
It would also create jobs during the global financial crisis, which gave the timetable

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.000
a second kind of urgency.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.960
Planners calculated that added depth and weirs could meet irrigation needs and peak

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:34.540
flow storage without requiring new land acquisition on the scale of the rejected canal.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.680
The canal had asked the public for a different river.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.440
This plan asked the same rivers to hold more water in place.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.160
Between late 2008 and June 2009, Lee issued repeated design orders.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:52.340
Those orders raised the minimum depth on the Nakhdon River from 4 meters to 6 meters.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:58.200
years. They also set a total storage target of 800 million tons. Construction crews installed

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.900
16 large concrete weirs and performed extensive dredging. The work was complete by 2012. Early

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:09.580
government statements highlighted reinforced banks and new water reserves as immediate gains.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:14.700
Some local officials reported fewer minor flood events in the first year after completion.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:20.740
Those reports mattered. A project sold on flood protection had in places, something to

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:26.120
point to. Skeptics in environmental groups and in parts of the Environment Ministry warned

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:31.280
that the weirs would reduce flow velocity. Those concerns were set aside during the accelerated

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.380
timetable. The Board of Audit and Inspection later documented that Ministry staff had

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:41.920
raised the risk of green tides, yet were directed not to emphasize it. The compressed schedule

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.660
had another consequence for the science itself. Hydraulic modeling of the new minimum

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:51.640
depth was completed only after the presidential directives had already fixed the storage volumes.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:57.000
The numbers were locked. The picture of how the water would actually move arrived afterward.

00:02:57.958 --> 00:03:03.378
Once the weirs held water at the new depths, flow on the Naktong dropped to roughly one tenth of

00:03:03.378 --> 00:03:08.478
its prior speed in many reaches. A river that used to turn over in a day could now linger.

00:03:09.178 --> 00:03:14.398
Lower velocity allowed nutrients to remain in place. Biochemical oxygen demand and

00:03:14.398 --> 00:03:18.898
and phytoplankton levels rose above the five-year average recorded before 2009.

00:03:19.838 --> 00:03:25.618
The Board of Audit and Inspections 2013 and 2018 reports recorded higher algae concentrations

00:03:25.978 --> 00:03:30.198
and chemical oxygen demand across every reservoir the project had created.

00:03:30.898 --> 00:03:34.738
Visible blooms that people came to call a green latte appeared each summer.

00:03:35.518 --> 00:03:40.438
The 2014 Prime Minister's office panel confirmed that stagnation from the structures and the

00:03:40.438 --> 00:03:41.878
dredging was the direct cause.

00:03:41.878 --> 00:03:48.098
The same panel noted that flood protection had improved in 93.7% of surveyed areas.

00:03:48.618 --> 00:03:50.438
Both findings could be true at once.

00:03:51.038 --> 00:03:54.038
The water was safer from some peaks and poorer as water.

00:03:54.578 --> 00:03:58.718
Because the weirs could not be operated to restore natural flushing without losing the

00:03:58.718 --> 00:04:03.798
storage the president had ordered, operators faced a recurring choice between water quantity

00:04:03.798 --> 00:04:04.938
and water quality.

00:04:05.558 --> 00:04:09.078
Open the gates and the reserve the directive required began to drain.

00:04:09.078 --> 00:04:14.198
remain. Keep them shut and the stillness remained. Downstream communities that had expected

00:04:14.198 --> 00:04:19.178
cleaner irrigation water instead dealt with treatment costs and periodic restrictions during

00:04:19.178 --> 00:04:24.518
bloom events. The same slowed water that reduced some flood peaks also prevented the natural

00:04:24.518 --> 00:04:29.658
scouring that had once limited nutrient build-up. What the current no longer carried away

00:04:29.658 --> 00:04:35.698
stayed. Season after season, for the next warm stretch to feed on. Over successive seasons,

00:04:35.698 --> 00:04:39.998
the ecological parks built beside the rivers required repeated interventions.

00:04:40.258 --> 00:04:45.198
The altered hydrology no longer supported the vegetation planners had assumed would thrive.

00:04:45.758 --> 00:04:50.258
The 2014 panel further observed that water leaks at six knock-donged weirs

00:04:50.258 --> 00:04:55.338
and faulty gates at 12 others compounded the difficulty of any corrective flushing.

00:04:55.898 --> 00:04:58.778
Repairs themselves demanded stable reservoir levels.

00:04:59.278 --> 00:05:02.518
To fix the hardware operators had to keep the water sitting still.

00:05:02.518 --> 00:05:07.758
Environmental groups documented fish kills and shifts in benthic invertebrate communities

00:05:07.758 --> 00:05:10.238
that had not appeared in pre-project monitoring.

00:05:10.838 --> 00:05:16.418
Those effects traced to the sustained low oxygen conditions, rather than to any single typhoon.

00:05:16.978 --> 00:05:18.978
The harm was not a storm that came and went.

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:21.078
It was a new normal in the channel.

00:05:22.146 --> 00:05:28.006
Multiple audits by the Board of Audit and Inspection and the 2014 Investigative Commission documented

00:05:28.006 --> 00:05:33.846
the water quality decline and structural issues at 15 of the 16 weirs. Successive

00:05:33.846 --> 00:05:39.286
administrations face pressure from civic groups to open gates more frequently, or to remove structures.

00:05:39.946 --> 00:05:44.886
Storage targets limited how far those measures could go. Some weirs now operate under

00:05:44.886 --> 00:05:49.726
adjusted schedules that trade a portion of the original water reserve for modest flow increases.

00:05:50.506 --> 00:05:55.526
The partial fixes have not restored pre-project oxygen levels, and they have not eliminated

00:05:55.526 --> 00:05:56.666
the seasonal blooms.

00:05:57.146 --> 00:06:01.986
Public debate continues over whether further modification or full removal would be required

00:06:01.986 --> 00:06:04.126
to address the persistent utrification.

00:06:04.846 --> 00:06:07.906
Proposals to dismantle individual weirs have been studied.

00:06:08.566 --> 00:06:12.946
Each study must still reconcile the original storage commitment with the oxygen deficits

00:06:12.946 --> 00:06:14.466
the monitoring has recorded.

00:06:15.086 --> 00:06:17.966
The political clock that hurried the build has a long echo.

00:06:18.626 --> 00:06:23.066
Every later government inherits a number it did not set, and a river that now behaves

00:06:23.066 --> 00:06:24.226
according to that number.

00:06:24.226 --> 00:06:29.342
When a single leader sets numeric targets for depth and storage without an independent

00:06:29.342 --> 00:06:34.402
review of flow consequences, the resulting structures reward volume over circulation.

00:06:35.102 --> 00:06:38.642
The metric that can be announced is the metric the concrete is built to serve.

00:06:39.242 --> 00:06:43.882
When an environment ministry is instructed to suppress its own risk assessments, later

00:06:43.882 --> 00:06:48.502
audits must reconstruct the original warnings from internal records rather than act on them

00:06:48.502 --> 00:06:49.222
in real time.

00:06:49.802 --> 00:06:51.222
The knowledge was in the building.

00:06:51.702 --> 00:06:53.542
It was not allowed to change the design.

00:06:53.542 --> 00:06:59.302
When construction proceeds on a fixed political timetable, the completed system locks in operating

00:06:59.302 --> 00:07:02.862
rules that cannot easily adapt once monitoring data arrive.

00:07:03.382 --> 00:07:08.262
By then the gates, the leaks, and the storage promise are all arguing with the oxygen readings.

00:07:09.062 --> 00:07:13.482
Environmental intelligence on this network keeps a longer watch on how water rules get

00:07:13.482 --> 00:07:16.722
written, and on what those rules do after the ribbon is cut.

00:07:17.362 --> 00:07:21.942
How might today's large river or reservoir projects build an explicit test of minimum

00:07:21.942 --> 00:07:25.082
flow velocity before final design approval is granted.

00:07:25.562 --> 00:07:26.822
That's the case for today.

00:07:27.342 --> 00:07:29.682
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them.

00:07:30.142 --> 00:07:34.262
So send this one to someone who'd find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

